
📘 CS-1202 · A Bin of Parts
What We Learned From This Topic
🎯 Learning Outcomes
From Lists, we learned how Python stores and manages a collection of values that can be accessed, changed, added, removed, and reordered.

1. Understanding Lists
A list is a collection of multiple values.

Lists are created using square brackets [ ].

A list can contain different types of data.

parts = [25.02, 24.98, 25.05]

mixed = ["PE-26-0137", 25.01, True]

2. Indexing & Slicing
Lists use the same zero-based indexing as strings.

Negative indexing also works.

Lists can be sliced using [start:stop].

Start is included, stop is excluded.

parts[0]      # First element

parts[-1]     # Last element

parts[0:2]    # First two elements

3. Lists Are Mutable ⭐
The biggest difference from strings:

A list is mutable, while a string is immutable.

We can directly change an element:

parts[0] = 25.01

This works because lists can be modified after creation.

4. Adding Elements
We learned two important methods:

parts.append(25.10)

Adds an element at the end.

parts.insert(1, 24.90)

Adds an element at a specific index and shifts later elements to the right.

5. Removing Elements
We learned two different ways to remove items:

pop() → removes by position

parts.pop(0)

remove() → removes by value

parts.remove(24.98)

⭐ Important Difference
pop() = remove by index
remove() = remove by value

Also, pop() returns the removed element, while remove() returns None.

6. Reordering a List
parts.sort()

parts.reverse()

sort() → arranges elements in order.

reverse() → reverses the order.

Both modify the existing list.

7. Index Renumbering
After an element is removed, the remaining elements shift positions.

Example:

Before: [A, B, C, D]

Index:   0  1  2  3


Remove B


After:  [A, C, D]

Index:   0  1  2

Important: Never assume an element keeps its old index after insertion or deletion.

